influence.description:
An influence edge records genuine design lineage from source_family_id to this family -- not general co-occurrence in the same paper. Record one only when at least one of the following holds, and always cite what specifically was adopted:

1. Concrete component reuse -- the same S-box, key schedule, round function, permutation, whitening/FX construction, or round constants appear in both designs. Example: Whirlpool reuses the AES S-box unchanged inside its internal cipher W.
2. Explicit lineage language about the design itself -- the source states the design is 'based on,' 'derived from,' 'inspired by,' 'generalizes,' or 'extends' an earlier one. Example: KASUMI's own specification describes it as a direct modification of MISTY1's structure.
3. Overlapping authorship with an acknowledged design connection -- at least one author of the new design is also an author of the earlier one (a full shared team is not required), and a connection between the two is acknowledged, even briefly and even if the structures otherwise differ. Example: Joan Daemen co-designed both Rijndael/AES and (later) Keccak; the two are recorded as related on that basis even though Keccak's structure otherwise departs entirely from AES's byte-oriented SPN.
4. Well-documented historical continuity, even if unstated in the primary source -- the official spec has no design-rationale section (or is silent on ancestry), but reliable secondary sources (surveys, textbooks, established cryptographic history) document a specific, concrete relationship between these two named designs -- not merely that both belong to the same general category or paradigm -- with a clear chronological gap. Example: DES's own FIPS 46 text contains no design rationale, but it is well documented that DES is a weakened, NSA-modified version of IBM's Lucifer cipher, not merely 'another Feistel cipher.'

Do not record an edge, even if the source discusses the relationship at length, when it is only:
- a performance/benchmark comparison ('faster than X', 'we compare cycle counts to X');
- 'positioned against'/'designed to avoid a weakness of X' framing where the new design is otherwise independent and does not retain or adapt X's concrete structure. If instead the new design keeps X's structure largely intact and specifically patches a found weakness in it, that is concrete component reuse (see 1), not this exclusion -- e.g. XTEA correcting TEA's key-schedule weakness, XXTEA in turn correcting XTEA's;
- a shared target application or use case ('also lightweight');
- a shared abstract paradigm with no concretely borrowed mechanism ('both ARX', 'both Feistel', 'both sponge-based') -- even when the source self-categorizes this way, and even when a chronologically earlier example of the same paradigm exists. For sponge/duplex designs specifically: if only the abstract Absorb/Squeeze paradigm is borrowed (not a concrete permutation), add 'sponge_construction' to the family's construction_ids instead of inventing an influence edge to whichever cipher popularized sponges;
- a related-work listing enumerating several prior designs sharing a generic technique, without singling one out as the actual basis;
- anything the source states was developed independently.

When unsure, omit the edge -- a missed influence is far cheaper to add later than a fabricated one is to catch.
